# XBD 1 Introduction
# Copyright (c) 2026 Nicholas Martin. All rights reserved.
#
# The chapter states the scope of the standard, how its words (shall,
# should, may, can, implementation-defined, undefined, unspecified, legacy)
# are used, what it refers to, and the margin codes that mark options. None
# of it is a behaviour of a system under test: the words decide how every
# other assertion is written, and the margin codes are the option names of
# core/options.list, used in the "when" fields of the assertions.

assertion  XBD.1-introduction.001
anchor     basedefs/V1_chap01.html#1.6
anchor     basedefs/V1_chap01.html#1.8
class      untestable
since      1992
says       The chapter defines the standard's wording and the option codes in
           its margins; it adds no requirement on a running system.
reason     Terminology, scope, references and notation; the only requirement
           it states for implementations, that implementation-defined
           behaviour be documented, concerns documents and is accounted for
           in every module that meets such a choice.
